  "difficulty": 3,
  "marks": 1,
  "estimatedSeconds": 90,
  "stem": "The lifetime $T$ hours of a fuse has an exponential distribution with mean $500$ hours. A fuse has already lasted $250$ hours. What is the probability that it lasts at least another $150$ hours?",
  "options": [
   {
    "key": "A",
    "content": "$\\mathrm{e}^{-0.8} \\approx 0.449$",
    "rationale": "This is P(T > 400), which ignores the information that the fuse has already lasted 250 hours."
   },
   {
    "key": "B",
    "content": "$1 - \\mathrm{e}^{-0.3} \\approx 0.259$",
    "rationale": "This is the probability that it fails within the next 150 hours."
   },
   {
    "key": "C",
    "content": "$\\mathrm{e}^{-0.3} \\approx 0.741$",
    "correct": true,
    "rationale": "Correct: by the memoryless property, $\\mathrm{P}(T > 400 \\mid T > 250) = \\mathrm{P}(T > 150) = \\mathrm{e}^{-150/500}$."
   },
   {
    "key": "D",
    "content": "$\\mathrm{e}^{-0.5} \\approx 0.607$",
    "rationale": "This is P(T > 250), the probability it has survived so far, not the further 150 hours."
   }
  ],
  "explanation": "$\\mathrm{P}(T > 400 \\mid T > 250) = \\frac{\\mathrm{e}^{-400/500}}{\\mathrm{e}^{-250/500}} = \\mathrm{e}^{-150/500} = 0.741$.",
  "hints": [
