  "difficulty": 4,
  "marks": 4,
  "estimatedSeconds": 360,
  "stem": "$X$ has pdf $f(x) = \\lambda\\mathrm{e}^{-\\lambda x}$ for $x \\ge 0$. Use integration by parts to show that $\\mathrm{E}(X) = \\dfrac{1}{\\lambda}$.",
  "answerSpec": {
   "markScheme": {
    "points": [
     {
      "id": "p1",
      "description": "M1 writes E(X) = ∫ xλe^(−λx) dx from 0 to ∞ and chooses u = x, dv/dx = λe^(−λx).",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "id": "p2",
      "description": "A1 obtains [−xe^(−λx)] + ∫ e^(−λx) dx.",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "id": "p3",
      "description": "B1 states the boundary term is 0 (xe^(−λx) → 0 as x → ∞, and 0 at x = 0).",
      "marks": 1
     },
     {
      "id": "p4",
      "description": "A1 evaluates ∫ e^(−λx) dx from 0 to ∞ = 1/λ.",
      "marks": 1
     }
    ]
   },
   "modelAnswer": "$$\n\\mathrm{E}(X) = \\int_0^\\infty x\\lambda\\mathrm{e}^{-\\lambda x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x = \\left[-x\\mathrm{e}^{-\\lambda x}\\right]_0^\\infty + \\int_0^\\infty \\mathrm{e}^{-\\lambda x}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n$$\n$$\n= 0 + \\left[-\\frac{1}{\\lambda}\\mathrm{e}^{-\\lambda x}\\right]_0^\\infty = 0 - \\left(-\\frac{1}{\\lambda}\\right) = \\frac{1}{\\lambda}\n$$"
  },
  "markingMode": "MANUAL",
  "explanation": "The boundary term vanishes because the exponential decays faster than x grows.",
  "hints": [
   "Take u = x so that du/dx = 1."
  ],
  "skill": "ANALYSIS",
  "calculator": false
 },
